+## [0.8.2+nodecollab.6] - 2026-10-26
+
+Local build on top of 0.8.2+nodecollab.5, a client upgrade for centers that
+run nodecollab.3; not an upstream tag. Refs, commits, payloads and remote
+calls per operation are unchanged; only the `runtime` value differs, so .3,
+.5 and .6 agents can share a center.
+
+### Fixed
+
+- Claims that follow the agent's own completion lost more compare-and-swaps
+  under nodecollab.5 than under .3 (center bench: 1.2–1.3 receive-hook runs
+  per claim against 1.0–1.1; completions unchanged). A published commit
+  makes every writer whose transaction was in flight lose and redo at once;
+  their redos read the new state within about 50 ms and land about one
+  attempt later. Because opening storage no longer fetches, a command-line
+  agent's claim reached its own fetch 0.3–0.5 s sooner after its completion,
+  inside that interval: it read the same state as the redos and lost to one
+  of them, often after the receive hook had already checked it. A write a
+  cache starts more than 50 ms after its own publish, while other writers
+  are active, now waits until the publishing attempt's duration plus 50 ms
+  has passed (at most 2 seconds). Writes started at once in the same process
+  are ahead of the redos and still go immediately. In a local copy of the
+  center's staggered 20-agent bench (center scripts per operation, guard on
+  nodecollab.3, its seed, 80 ms round trips; first minute excluded), claims
+  averaged 1.62 guard runs with nodecollab.5, 1.26 with this change and 1.20
+  with nodecollab.3; claim p95 6.51 s, 4.72 s and 4.99 s. The median claim
+  is back at nodecollab.3's 1.97 s (nodecollab.5: 1.48 s); renewals and
+  connections per claim keep the nodecollab.5 savings.
+- A redo after a lost compare-and-swap parsed the whole event log again: the
+  recorded chain head included the discarded append. The head before that
+  append is now kept as well, so only the winner's lines are parsed.
+
+### Added
+
+- `tests/acceptance/git_storage_back_to_back.py`: compare-and-swap attempts
+  of writes that follow the writer's own publish, immediately or after a
+  gap, with the yield on or off.
+- Interop cases against the nodecollab.5 wheel (`CASCADE_NODECOLLAB5_WHEEL`):
+  mixed .5 and .6 agents claiming, renewing, completing and contesting one
+  task, on a plain remote and behind the center guard; a claim renewed and
+  completed alternately by .5 and .6 in one cache.
+
+## [0.8.2+nodecollab.5] - 2026-10-25
+
+Local build on top of 0.8.2+nodecollab.3, the client upgrade for centers that
+run nodecollab.3; not an upstream tag. The refs, commits, payloads and remote
+calls per operation are those of nodecollab.3; only the `runtime` value
+differs. nodecollab.4 is reserved.
+
+### Added
+
+- `tests/integration/test_git_storage_nc3_interop.py` against the
+  nodecollab.3 wheel (`CASCADE_NODECOLLAB3_WHEEL`): identical heartbeat and
+  state formats apart from the runtime marker, heartbeats of each runtime
+  honoured by the other, mixed agents contending on one namespace, and, with
+  `CASCADE_CENTER_GUARD`, this runtime's writes accepted by
+  CascadeCenter-web's receive guard running on nodecollab.3 with
+  `--require-runtime`.
+- The remote-call comparison counts construction too and adds a
+  nodecollab.3 column when `CASCADE_NODECOLLAB3_WHEEL` is set.
+
+### Changed
+
+- Constructing `GitStorage` no longer synchronizes: it runs no Git command
+  and reads no state. A renewal in a new process with a warm cache is now
+  one remote call (the heartbeat push) and five Git commands in all
+  (`for-each-ref`, `cat-file`, `commit-tree`, `push`, `update-ref`); it
+  fetches only when the push shows the cache was outdated. A write in a new
+  process is one fetch and one push instead of two fetches and one push.
+- Shorter window between reading the state and pushing on it:
+  - a redo after a lost compare-and-swap moves the working copy from the lost
+    commit to the winner's with one two-tree `read-tree` instead of checking
+    out every file;
+  - the next process trusts a clean working copy recorded in
+    `<cache>/git-state.clean` instead of rehashing it;
+  - the event log's chain head is kept with a SHA-256 of the bytes it covers
+    (`<cache>/git-state.events-head` for Git), so an append parses only the
+    lines after that prefix;
+  - the base tree comes with the cached refs, the empty tree OID is known
+    without `mktree`, and namespace validation needs no Git process;
+  - fetches skip Git's automatic maintenance, which would run inside the
+    window; it runs after a successful publish when the cache holds about
+    6700 loose objects or 50 packs.
+- Fairer redo under bursts: the wait before a redo is drawn below
+  `min(50 ms * 2^(attempt-1), 400 ms, 400 ms * exp(-waited / 1.5 s))`, at
+  least 10 ms. Writers that have waited longest redo first; newcomers still
+  back off up to 400 ms. In a simulation of the race this cut p99 write
+  latency by about 20% and raised attempts per second by about 20%. The
+  60-second deadline and `STATE_CONFLICT` result are unchanged.
+
+### Fixed
+
+- Redo of a lost compare-and-swap could stop after 1000 attempts before the
+  60-second deadline once fast attempts waited only the 10 ms floor; the
+  deadline now ends it.
+- `FileEventStore.emit` chained each event to the head it had read when it
+  was created, so a store kept across another process's append could fork the
+  chain. It now reads the head on disk before every append.
+- In-process cancel notifiers registered with a Git storage's token store are
+  kept across state changes; the file layer is no longer rebuilt per commit.
+
+### Breaking changes
+
+- `GitStorage.state_oid` is `None` until the first `lock()`,
+  `read_snapshot()` or `renewal_view()`; `load()`, `exists()` and the
+  components used outside those read the local cache as last synchronized
+  instead of the state at construction.
+- `StorageProtocol.events`, `tokens`, `ops`, `content` and `heartbeats` are
+  read-only properties. Backends that set plain attributes still conform;
+  code that assigned `GitStorage.events` and the like must stop.
+- `FileEventStore` no longer has `_last_hash` and reads nothing at
+  construction. `FileStorage.refresh()` makes it read its clock from
+  `graph.json` again on next use.
+- State commits and heartbeat payloads carry `runtime=0.8.2+nodecollab.5`. A
+  receive hook must accept it; CascadeCenter-web's guard accepts every
+  nodecollab.N with N ≥ 3. Scripts that require the installed version to
+  equal `0.8.2+nodecollab.3` must accept `.5`.
+
+## [0.8.2+nodecollab.3] - 2026-10-18
+
+Local build on top of 0.8.2+nodecollab.1; not an upstream tag. It includes the
+internal, unreleased nodecollab.2 read path.
+
+### Added
+
+- Per-claim heartbeat refs `refs/heads/cascade-heartbeat/<ns>/<sha256(task_id)>`.
+  A claim creates its heartbeat in the same atomic push as the state; renewing
+  is one compare-and-swap push of that ref, with no state write and no event.
+  Several heartbeats can be renewed in one atomic push.
+- The effective lease, `max(claimed_at, heartbeat.renewed_at)` with the
+  heartbeat's timeout for a heartbeat of the current claim only, drives claim
+  reclamation, `check-timeouts`, completion checks of published tasks and the
+  `renewed_at` / `lease_expires_at` fields of `list-nodes` and `check`.
+  `Heartbeat`, `heartbeat_key` and the `Node.lease_*` methods expose it.
+- `read_snapshot()` and `renewal_view()` on every storage backend, and a
+  `heartbeats` component (`HeartbeatStoreProtocol`). Git storage reads one
+  state commit without writing anything remote; File and Redis use their lock.
+  Client queries read through `read_snapshot()`.
+- `STATE_CONFLICT` result code and `StateConflictError`.
+- `cascade.verification`: `verify_log` checks a whole event log against its
+  snapshot; `verify_appended` continues from a `LogCheckpoint` of a verified
+  log and checks only appended events, with the same conclusion as a full
+  check (falling back to it when the verified bytes or artifacts changed).
+  `verify_storage` applies both to a storage; checkpoints serialize with
+  `to_dict()` / `from_dict()`. `check_chain`, `replay_from` and
+  `compare_snapshot` are the shared building blocks; `events.read_bytes()`
+  returns the stored log.
+- `list-nodes` and `check` return unusable heartbeats in `heartbeat_errors`;
+  `storage.heartbeats.malformed()` lists them.
+- [Git heartbeat refs](docs/git-heartbeat-refs.md): ref format, payload and the
+  rules for a server receive hook, plus the switch and upgrade order.
+
+### Changed
+
+- Git writes no longer take a lease or touch `cascade-lock/<ns>`. A transaction
+  fetches once and publishes the state commit plus its heartbeat changes with
+  one atomic expected-OID push; a lost compare-and-swap is redone on fresh
+  state with jittered backoff (50 ms doubling to 400 ms) for up to 60 seconds,
+  then fails with `STATE_CONFLICT`, having published nothing. Compaction is a
+  compare-and-swap too.
+- Renewal no longer writes `TASK_RENEWED`; historical ones still replay.
+- A renewal after the lease expired is refused for every task: the claim is
+  released and `STALE_TOKEN` returned. Previously only published tasks were
+  refused and an expired unpublished claim was revived.
+- Every synchronization is one fetch. Warm-cache remote calls drop from 5 to 1
+  per read, 6 to 2 per write and 6 to 1 per renewal (see
+  [Git storage](docs/git-storage.md#runtime-contract)).
+- A clean Git working copy is reused without checkout and a state change
+  rewrites only changed files.
+- Lease waits of the standalone `GitRefLease` back off from 0.1 to 2 seconds
+  with jitter and fetch a holder's payload once per lease commit.
+
+### Fixed
+
+- A client killed inside a write or during its push no longer blocks other
+  writers until a lease expires.
+- Cancel notifications fire only after the transaction's commit lands.
+- Timed-out pushes are classified by reading the refs back.
+
+### Breaking changes
+
+- `GitStorage` no longer accepts `lease_seconds` or
+  `clock_skew_tolerance_seconds`; there is no transaction lease.
+- `GitStorage.lock()` is an optimistic transaction and may raise
+  `StateConflictError` (a `LockContentionError`) when another writer published
+  first. `CascadeClient` methods redo internally and return `STATE_CONFLICT`
+  after 60 seconds.
+- Git synchronization is always one `git fetch` of the state and heartbeat
+  refs (incremental after the first, depth one only for a fresh cache) instead
+  of `ls-remote` followed by a fetch when something moved.
+- The Git working copy moved from `<cache>/state-repo` to `<cache>/git-state`;
+  the first run of this version fetches the newest snapshot once. The older
+  `state-repo` and `lease.git` are left alone.
+- An expired lease can no longer be renewed for unpublished tasks either.
+- `StorageProtocol` gained `heartbeats`, `read_snapshot()` and
+  `renewal_view()`, and `EventStoreProtocol` gained `read_bytes()`; custom
+  backends must add them.
+
+### Compatibility
+
+- Fencing, the event hash chain, replay, corruption handling and op receipts
+  are unchanged.
+- Older runtimes compute expiry from `claimed_at` only and would reclaim claims
+  kept alive by heartbeats. Install a heartbeat-aware receive hook first, then
+  upgrade every participant, then refuse older writers; see the upgrade order
+  in [Git heartbeat refs](docs/git-heartbeat-refs.md).
+- The Git remote must support atomic pushes (stock Git 2.4 or later).
+- The coordination repository must set `receive.autogc false`: a detached
+  automatic repack beside concurrent pushes dropped a just-referenced object
+  in testing. Cascade's own caches now run auto-maintenance in the foreground.
+
