+# Git heartbeat refs: interface for receive hooks (0.8.2+nodecollab.3 and later)
+
+From 0.8.2+nodecollab.3, Git storage renews leases with one heartbeat ref
+per ACTIVE claim and writes state without a lease ref. This document is the
+contract a server-side receive hook can enforce. The runtime helpers named
+below are in `cascade.types` and `cascade.core.node`, so a hook running the
+same runtime can use them instead of reimplementing the rules.
+
+0.8.2+nodecollab.5 and .6 write exactly the same refs and objects; only the
+`runtime` value differs. "A pinned runtime" below means a runtime string the
+hook accepts. A hook should accept `0.8.2+nodecollab.N` for every N ≥ 3 (as
+CascadeCenter-web's `cascade-node-receive.py` does), so agents can move
+between heartbeat runtimes without a server change.
+
+## Refs
+
+| Ref | Written by 0.8.2+nodecollab.3 and later |
+|-----|-------------------------------|
+| `refs/heads/cascade-state/<ns>` | every state change, with an expected OID |
+| `refs/heads/cascade-heartbeat/<ns>/<key>` | claim, renewal, and the end of a claim |
+| `refs/heads/cascade-archive/<ns>/<oid-prefix>` | compaction only |
+| `refs/heads/cascade-lock/<ns>` | never read or written |
+
+`<key>` is the lowercase hexadecimal SHA-256 of the task ID in UTF-8
+(`heartbeat_key(task_id)`, 64 characters). Task IDs may contain `..` or end in
+`.lock`, so they never appear in a ref name.
+
+## Heartbeat commit
+
+- Tree: the empty tree.
+- Parents: none when the ref is created; exactly the ref's previous value when
+  it is updated.
+- Commit object: at most 4096 bytes; the message (payload) at most 3584 bytes.
+- Message: one compact JSON object with exactly these keys:
+
+| Key | Type | Meaning |
+|-----|------|---------|
+| `namespace` | string, 1–256 | the coordination namespace |
+| `task_id` | task ID slug | the claimed task |
+| `agent_id` | string, 1–256 | the claim's executor |
+| `claim_token` | integer ≥ 1 (not boolean) | the claim's fencing token |
+| `renewed_at` | finite number, Unix seconds | time of this renewal |
+| `timeout` | finite number > 0, or null | lease length from `renewed_at` |
+| `runtime` | string | the writer's version, `0.8.2+nodecollab.N` with N ≥ 3 |
+
+`Heartbeat.from_payload` enforces the keys and types. A malformed payload, a
+payload of another namespace, or one whose task does not hash to the ref key
+counts as no heartbeat; the runtime reports it in `heartbeat_errors` of
+`list-nodes` and `check` and in `storage.heartbeats.malformed()`.
+
+## Effective lease
+
+A heartbeat applies only while the node is ACTIVE and its `task_id`,
+`agent_id` and `claim_token` equal the node's current claim
+(`Node.heartbeat_applies`). Then:
+
+```text
+renewed_at = max(node.claimed_at, heartbeat.renewed_at)
+timeout    = heartbeat.timeout
+expired    = now >= renewed_at + timeout          (never when timeout is null)
+```
+
+Without an applicable heartbeat, `node.claimed_at` and `node.timeout` are used
+as before. `Node.lease_renewed_at`, `Node.lease_timeout`,
+`Node.lease_expires_at` and `Node.is_timed_out(now, heartbeat)` implement
+exactly this. Claim reclamation, `check-timeouts`, the expiry checks of
+complete/release/fail on published tasks, queries and the expiry of the
+renewal itself all use the effective lease. A heartbeat of an older claim
+(another token or agent) is ignored everywhere.
+
+Historical `TASK_RENEWED` events still replay: they move `claimed_at` and
+`timeout` as before. This runtime never writes them.
+
+## Pushes this runtime makes
+
+Every push is `git push --atomic`, and every refspec carries an expected OID.
+
+| Operation | Refs in the push |
+|-----------|------------------|
+| Claim | state (appends `TASK_CLAIMED`) and creates the claim's heartbeat with `renewed_at == claimed_at` and the claim's `timeout`; the expected value is absent or a stale heartbeat of an older claim |
+| Renewal | the heartbeat ref of each renewed claim, nothing else; one push may renew several claims |
+| Complete, release, fail, timeout, rework, reclaim in claim | state, and deletes the heartbeat of every claim the transaction ended |
+| Any other state change | state, and may delete heartbeats whose claim is not ACTIVE in the new state |
+| Compaction | state (new root commit) and the archive ref |
+
+A state commit message contains `runtime=<version>` on its own line, for
+example `runtime=0.8.2+nodecollab.5`.
+
+## Verifying a push incrementally
+
+`cascade.verification` verifies only what a push appended: keep the
+`LogCheckpoint` of the last accepted state OID (`LogCheckpoint.to_dict()`),
+and for an incoming state commit call `verify_appended(checkpoint,
+<new events.jsonl bytes>, <new snapshot>, <new content>)`. The result is the
+one a full `verify_log` would give for the same bytes. `prefix_preserved` is
+false when the accepted history was rewritten, reordered or truncated, which
+rule 15 refuses. The rules below that compare individual events still apply
+to the appended events.
+
+## Rules a receive hook should enforce
+
+Validate all lines of one push together and refuse the whole push if any rule
+fails. "New state" means the state commit of this push when the push updates
+the state ref, otherwise the current state ref.
+
+Heartbeat create or update (`new` is not zero):
+
+1. The ref is `refs/heads/cascade-heartbeat/<ns>/<64 hex>` of this namespace.
+2. `new` is a commit of at most 4096 bytes with the empty tree. Its parents are
+   none when `old` is zero and exactly `[old]` otherwise.
+3. The payload passes `Heartbeat.from_payload`; `namespace` is this namespace,
+   `runtime` is a pinned runtime, and `heartbeat_key(task_id)` is the key.
+4. In the new state the task exists and is ACTIVE, and its `agent_id` and
+   `claim_token` equal the payload's.
+5. The SSH writer is the payload's `agent_id`.
+6. `abs(renewed_at - server_time) <= 60`, and `timeout` is null or finite
+   between 1 and 86400 seconds; a published task (`task_input` present) needs
+   a non-null timeout.
+7. Creation: either this push appends `TASK_CLAIMED` for the task with the
+   same token, `renewed_at == claimed_at` and the same timeout, or the claim
+   has no heartbeat yet and `node.is_timed_out(server_time)` is false.
+8. Update of the same claim's heartbeat: `renewed_at` does not decrease, and
+   `node.is_timed_out(server_time, old_heartbeat)` is false, so a renewal
+   cannot revive an expired lease.
+
+Heartbeat delete (`new` is zero):
+
+9. Only in a push that also updates the state ref, and only when the deleted
+   payload's claim is not ACTIVE in the new state (missing task, other state,
+   or another agent or token). This allows the owner's completion and any
+   writer's timeout reclaim or cleanup, and nothing else.
+
+State ref update:
+
+10. The commit message has the line `runtime=<version>` with a pinned runtime.
+11. Expiry checks use the task's heartbeat as it was before this push: the
+    `old` OID of its deletion line in this push, otherwise the current
+    heartbeat ref. Completion, release and failure of a published task require
+    `not node.is_timed_out(server_time, heartbeat)`; `TASK_TIMED_OUT` requires
+    `node.is_timed_out(server_time, heartbeat)`.
+12. `TASK_RENEWED` is no longer appended.
+13. A push that ends a claim also deletes that claim's heartbeat if one exists.
+
+Other refs:
+
+14. Agents may write only the state ref and heartbeat refs of their namespace.
+    Pushes of `refs/heads/cascade-lock/<ns>` are refused after the switch.
+15. The new state's event log keeps the accepted log as an exact byte prefix
+    (`prefix_preserved`), and its verification is valid.
+
+## Server configuration
+
+Heartbeats make concurrent pushes normal. Turn off automatic maintenance
+after pushes in the coordination repository (`receive.autogc false`) and run
+maintenance only with writers paused; see the Git storage runtime contract
+for the corruption a detached repack caused under concurrent pushes.
+
+## Switch and upgrade order
+
+Older runtimes (0.8.2+nodecollab.1 and .2) do not read heartbeat refs. They
+compute expiry from `claimed_at` alone, so they reclaim live claims that are
+kept alive only by heartbeats, and they never delete heartbeat refs. This
+runtime must therefore never run against a center that still accepts older
+writers without the heartbeat-aware rules.
+
+1. Install a hook that applies rules 1–9, 11 and 13 to every push, while
+   still accepting older runtimes' state pushes, lock-ref pushes and
+   `TASK_RENEWED` events. In this phase an older agent that tries to reclaim
+   a heartbeat-extended claim has its whole push refused; its other writes
+   still work.
+2. Upgrade the center publisher and every agent to 0.8.2+nodecollab.3 or
+   later; nodecollab.3 and .5 can be mixed.
+3. Switch: also enforce rules 10, 12 and 14, which refuses every write of an
+   older runtime.
+
+With all agents stopped, the three steps can be done at once. Rolling back
+after step 3 means re-allowing older writers, which then do not see
+heartbeat-only renewals.
+
+Moving agents between nodecollab.3, .5 and .6, before or after step 3,
+needs no server change when rules 3 and 10 accept every nodecollab.N with
+N ≥ 3. Agents can be moved one at a time; agents of these runtimes honour
+each other's heartbeats and contend on the same refs.
