 ## Runtime contract
 
-- The transaction lease defaults to 120 seconds and renews every 40 seconds
-  until the state push completes.
-- Each lock records its clock-skew tolerance. A contender applies the larger
-  of its local value and the holder's recorded value; the default bound is five
-  seconds. Production hosts must keep wall clocks within that bound using NTP.
-- Local acquisition timeouts use a monotonic clock, so a wall-clock correction
-  does not shorten the caller's wait budget.
-- A failed push is followed by a remote ref read. Only an observed target OID
-  counts as success; an unchanged expected OID is a transport/policy failure,
-  and a third OID is a real CAS conflict.
-- State fetches use depth one. Read-only transactions compare trees and do not
-  create duplicate state commits.
-- A lost renewal aborts publication. A process killed before the state push
-  publishes nothing; a process killed after ref acceptance leaves a complete
-  recoverable snapshot. Stale transaction locks expire and remain fenced.
+This section describes 0.8.2+nodecollab.6, a client upgrade on top of
+nodecollab.3 like nodecollab.5. It writes the refs, commits and payloads of
+nodecollab.3, with `runtime=0.8.2+nodecollab.6` as the only difference, and
+makes the same remote calls per operation; it differs in local work, in
+construction and in redo timing (see the changelog). nodecollab.2 was an
+internal step and is not released; nodecollab.4 is reserved.
+
+Construction:
+
+- `GitStorage(...)` runs no Git command, makes no remote call and reads no
+  state. `lock()` and `read_snapshot()` synchronize first. `state_oid` is
+  `None` until one of them or `renewal_view()` ran. `exists()`, `load()` and
+  the components outside those see the local cache as last synchronized.
+
+Writes are optimistic:
+
+- There is no transaction lease and no lock ref. A transaction fetches the
+  state and heartbeat refs once, runs on that snapshot, and publishes with one
+  `git push --atomic` in which every refspec carries an expected OID: the state
+  commit plus the heartbeat refs of claims it created or ended.
+- If another writer published first, nothing of this transaction lands and
+  `StateConflictError` (a `LockContentionError`) is raised. `CascadeClient`
+  then redoes the whole operation on the latest state, for up to 60 seconds.
+  Before each redo it waits a random time below a ceiling that doubles from
+  50 ms to 400 ms over the attempts and also shrinks with the time the write
+  has already waited (`0.4 s * exp(-waited / 1.5 s)`, at least 10 ms). Under a
+  burst, writers that have waited longest therefore fetch first after each
+  published commit, while newcomers back off further. In a simulation of the
+  compare-and-swap race (2 to 20 writers, 0.1 to 0.6 s per attempt) this cut
+  p99 write latency by about 20% against nodecollab.3's backoff, raised the
+  median by about 10%, and raised attempts per second, each a fetch and a
+  push, by about 20%. Each attempt
+  recomputes timestamps, fencing tokens, reservation expiry and the op_id
+  receipt check; cancel notifications fire only after the commit lands. Out
+  of time, the client returns `STATE_CONFLICT` with nothing published.
+- A redo moves the working copy from the lost commit to the winner's with
+  one two-tree `read-tree`, rewriting only the files that differ; the event
+  log's chain head is continued from the bytes before the lost append, so
+  only the winner's lines are parsed.
+- After this cache publishes a state commit, writers that lost to it redo at
+  once: they read the new state after their first redo wait (at most 50 ms)
+  and land about one attempt later. A write this cache starts within those
+  50 ms is ahead of them and goes at once. One that starts later, while
+  other writers are active (someone else wrote since this cache's previous
+  commit), waits until the publishing attempt's duration plus 50 ms has
+  passed since the publish (at most 2 seconds), then fetches; started
+  earlier it would read the same state as those redos and lose to one of
+  them, often after the receive hook had already checked it. The typical
+  case is an agent that claims the next task in a new process right after
+  completing one. Reads, renewals and redos never wait, and a lone writer
+  never waits. `<cache>/git-state.published` records the last publish.
+- After a failed or unknown push (including a timeout) the refs are read back.
+  A state commit counts as published only when it is observed, directly or as
+  an ancestor of the current state. An unchanged state with unchanged
+  heartbeats is a transport or policy failure (`LockError`, `command failed`);
+  a moved ref is a conflict.
+- A client that dies at any point leaves nothing other writers wait for.
+  Killed before its push lands, it publishes nothing; after, its commit is
+  complete.
+- The remote's receive-pack must support atomic pushes (stock Git since 2.4).
+- The coordination repository must not run automatic maintenance after
+  pushes: set `git config receive.autogc false` in the bare repository. With
+  Git 2.54's default, every push starts a detached `git maintenance run
+  --auto`; during a burst of concurrent heartbeat pushes, one such geometric
+  repack (cruft expiry two weeks) dropped an object that a renewal had just
+  referenced, leaving a heartbeat ref pointing at a missing commit and
+  failing every later fetch. Run maintenance only while writers are paused and
+  check the repository with `git fsck` afterwards. Hosted services that run
+  their own maintenance safely are not affected. Cascade's local caches never
+  run maintenance in the background for the same reason. Their fetches skip
+  automatic maintenance, which would otherwise run between reading the state
+  and pushing on it; after a successful publish, when the cache holds about
+  6700 loose objects or 50 packs, `git maintenance run --auto` runs in the
+  foreground under the cache lock.
+- Compaction is a compare-and-swap too: the archive ref and the new root are
+  pushed atomically against the archived state, and a concurrent write makes it
+  retry.
+
+Heartbeats:
+
+- A claim creates `refs/heads/cascade-heartbeat/<ns>/<sha256(task_id)>` in the
+  same atomic push as the state. Renewing pushes one new heartbeat commit to
+  that ref only, with the previous heartbeat as parent and expected OID. It
+  writes no state and no event, so different tasks' renewals never conflict,
+  and one push can renew several claims at once.
+- A renewal is validated against the locally cached state and heartbeat,
+  read from the cached commit objects with `for-each-ref` and `cat-file`; it
+  does not fetch or touch the working copy. With a warm cache a renewal in a
+  new process runs five Git commands, one of them a remote call (the push).
+  If the cached copy is outdated the push is refused and the renewal is
+  redone on freshly fetched data; a claim that ended meanwhile is reported as before
+  (`STALE_TOKEN`, `TASK_NOT_ACTIVE`). A heartbeat push that failed without
+  landing (transport or server error, refs read back unchanged) is repeated
+  the same way; after four attempts the last error is reported.
+- A lease that already expired is never renewed, whether or not the task is
+  published: the claim is released and the renewal returns `STALE_TOKEN`.
+- The effective renewal time is `max(claimed_at, heartbeat.renewed_at)` and the
+  lease length is the heartbeat's `timeout`, but only for a heartbeat whose
+  task, agent and token equal the node's current claim. Claim reclamation,
+  `check-timeouts`, completion checks, `list-nodes` (`renewed_at`,
+  `lease_expires_at`, `stale`) and `check` all use it.
+- Ending a claim deletes its heartbeat in the same atomic push, expecting the
+  OID it read. A renewal that lands first therefore makes a reclaim redo and
+  see the fresh lease. Heartbeats of older tokens are ignored and removed by
+  the next write. Historical `TASK_RENEWED` events still replay.
+- A heartbeat that cannot be used (malformed payload, another namespace, or a
+  task that does not belong under its ref) counts as absent for every expiry
+  decision. Reads report it: `list-nodes` and `check` return it in
+  `heartbeat_errors` with its key, ref, OID and reason, and say so in the
+  message; `storage.heartbeats.malformed()` lists them inside a view.
+- The ref format, payload and the rules a receive hook should enforce are in
+  [Git heartbeat refs](git-heartbeat-refs.md).
+
+Reads:
+
+- `GitStorage.read_snapshot()` holds only the local `.local-cache.lock`,
+  fetches once and serves graph, events, tokens, content and heartbeats from
+  one state commit (`GitStorage.state_oid`). It never pushes. `CascadeClient`
+  `nodes`, `check`, `history`, `show`, `diff`, `snapshot_at` and `load`, and the
+  `verify-chain` command, use it. A failed fetch raises `LockError`; a corrupt
+  snapshot raises `StorageCorruptionError` for that commit. Changing data
+  inside a read snapshot raises and publishes nothing.
+
+Verification:
+
+- `cascade.verification.verify_log(raw, snapshot, content)` verifies a whole
+  log (`storage.events.read_bytes()`): every line parses, the hash chain holds
+  from the first event, replay reproduces the snapshot (`replay.verify`
+  differences) and the edge contracts are equal. A passing result carries a
+  `LogCheckpoint`: event count, byte length and SHA-256 of the verified bytes,
+  chain head, replayed state, and digests of the artifact texts replay read.
+- `verify_appended(checkpoint, raw, snapshot, content)` returns the same
+  `conclusion()` as `verify_log` for the same input. When the log still starts
+  with the checkpoint's bytes and those artifacts are unchanged, it parses,
+  chains and replays only the appended lines; otherwise it verifies the whole
+  log and reports `prefix_preserved=False` when the verified bytes changed.
+  `consume=True` replays on the checkpoint's own state instead of a copy (about
+  10 times faster on large graphs) and makes that checkpoint unusable.
+- `verify_storage(storage, previous)` does both for a storage inside
+  `read_snapshot()` or `lock()`; for Git the checkpoint records `state_oid`.
+  `LogCheckpoint.to_dict()` / `from_dict()` keep a checkpoint across processes
+  (load only files you wrote). A receive hook or a center verifier keeps the
+  checkpoint of the last accepted OID and verifies each new state from it; a
+  renewal pushes no state and needs no verification.
+
+Local cache:
+
+- Every synchronization is one `git fetch` of the state ref and the heartbeat
+  refs. When nothing moved it transfers nothing and costs the same single
+  connection as `ls-remote`. A fresh cache fetches with depth one; later
+  fetches are incremental, so the shallow boundary does not grow.
+- The working copy is `<cache>/git-state`. Older runtimes' `state-repo` and
+  `lease.git` are left alone, so an older and this runtime may share a cache
+  directory without reusing each other's working copy.
+- A clean working copy is reused without checkout; a state change rewrites
+  only the files that changed. Any process about to change the working copy
+  first announces it in `<cache>/git-state.generation`, and a file-stat
+  fingerprint catches other edits, so a modified copy is always rebuilt from
+  the cached commit. `<cache>/git-state.clean` records the commit, generation
+  and fingerprint of a clean copy, so the next process trusts it without
+  rehashing the files.
+- `<cache>/git-state.events-head` records the byte length, SHA-256 and last
+  hash of the event log as last read. A later append parses only the lines
+  after a prefix with that digest; any other log is parsed in full.
+
+Remote Git calls per operation with a warm local cache, measured by
+`tests/integration/test_git_storage_compat.py` against the unchanged wheels
+(each call is one SSH login on an SSH remote):
+
+| Operation | nodecollab.1 | nodecollab.2 | nodecollab.3 | nodecollab.5 and .6 |
+|-----------|-------------:|-------------:|-------------:|-------------:|
+| Read, state unchanged | 5 | 1 | 1 | 1 (`fetch`) |
+| Read, state changed | 5 | 2 | 1 | 1 (`fetch`) |
+| Write, state unchanged | 6 | 3 | 2 | 2 (`fetch`, `push`) |
+| Write, state changed | 6 | 4 | 2 | 2 (`fetch`, `push`) |
+| Renewal | 6 | 3 | 1 | 1 (`push`) |
+| Renewal in a new process | 6 | 3 | 1 | 1 (`push`) |
+| Renewal in a new process, with construction | 8 | 4 | 2 | 1 (`push`) |
+| Write in a new process, with construction | 8 | 4 | 3 | 2 (`fetch`, `push`) |
+| Write while another process is in a transaction for 3 s | 18–48 | 8–10 | 2 | 2 |
+
+Through nodecollab.3, constructing `GitStorage` also synchronizes once (one
+`fetch`); from nodecollab.5 it makes no call (nodecollab.6 measured the
+same). The rows "with construction"
+count both, for a process that constructs the storage and runs one operation.
+
+Throughput limit: every write still appends to one hash-chained event log on
+one state ref, so writes are serialized at about one commit per
+fetch-to-push window. A burst of simultaneous writes from many agents is
+drained one commit at a time; see `tests/acceptance/git_storage_agent_load.py`
+for measured latencies under paced and burst load.
+
+Upgrade (summary; details and hook rules in
+[Git heartbeat refs](git-heartbeat-refs.md)):
+
+1. Install a heartbeat-aware receive hook that still accepts older runtimes.
+2. Upgrade the center publisher and every agent to nodecollab.3 or later.
+3. Switch the hook to refuse state commits without a `runtime=` marker of
